From: Erfan Abdi <erfangplus@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Kemu's fixes (kuloPo/platform_frameworks_libs_binary_translation): guest thread table, large frees, init trace

Three of the 17 commits the Kemu fork has over DigitalisX64's android16 (the same code base as this tree's base abefbe2b), each applied with its source
files only (their tests and Android.bp entries left out), a few include lines merged by hand:
- ae5358b1, fb941b4d (guest clone, guest threads): a guest popen/system/posix_spawn (CLONE_VFORK) and any child process that runs in the translator's
  memory (CLONE_VM without CLONE_THREAD) put GuestThreads into the thread table that nothing removed; once the kernel handed the same tid to a thread of
  ours, inserting it failed or found a dead thread's stack. Each GuestThread gets a serial and a process id; the vfork parent releases the child's
  entry, and a thread whose tid has an entry of another process releases it (GuestThreadMap::RemoveThreadIf, ReleaseVforkChild,
  ReleaseThreadOfGoneProcess).
- 8e1b5512 (free quarantine): a block above 1 MiB is freed at once, not kept in the 1024-entry ring (bounded at 1 GiB). The size is read after 0006's
  chunk check, not before: malloc_usable_size on an invalid pointer (PGR's frees) reads a header that is not there.
- 3b98206b: native_bridge_initialize logs after tracing is configured, so its line shows in BERBERIS_TRACING output.
Not taken: bc2dccd4 (guest ucontext on the guest stack: this tree's base already has it), the Vulkan layer entry points (6b760028: one of its four hunks
does not apply to this tree's native_bridge.cc and needs a rebase; it matters only for an app that ships its own Vulkan layer), and the
tiering/TBI/MOV-alias performance commits (a2a2de1a, b88517d9, 615e5c93, 4de3cab3: large changes to the heavy tier, where PGR's miscompile is; untested here).
The files keep their upstream Apache-2.0 headers; the Kemu fork's licence as a whole was not checked (docs/THIRD_PARTY.md). Applies after 0021. Module libberberis_arm64, libberberis_proxy_libc.

diff --git a/android_api/libc/free_quarantine.cc b/android_api/libc/free_quarantine.cc
--- a/android_api/libc/free_quarantine.cc
+++ b/android_api/libc/free_quarantine.cc
@@ -36,6 +36,9 @@
 // Bounded memory; each pointer is freed exactly once (just deferred). This brings
 // Berberis's free timing closer to real hardware so benign guest UAFs stay benign.
 
+// kuloPo/Kemu 8e1b5512 (apklayer 0022): allocation sizes for the large-block exemption below, without the fortify warning about allocator padding.
+#define __BIONIC_DISABLE_MALLOC_USABLE_SIZE_FORTIFY_WARNINGS
+#include <malloc.h>
 #include <stddef.h>
 
 #if defined(NATIVE_BRIDGE_GUEST_ARCH_ARM64)
@@ -237,6 +240,9 @@
 namespace {
 
 constexpr size_t kQuarantineSize = 1024;
+// kuloPo/Kemu 8e1b5512 (apklayer 0022): a block above this is freed at once, so the ring holds at most 1 GiB (1024 x 1 MiB) instead of unbounded
+// memory (a guest freeing many large buffers kept every one of them until it was evicted).
+constexpr size_t kMaxQuarantinedAllocationSize = 1024 * 1024;
 std::atomic<void*> g_quarantine[kQuarantineSize];
 std::atomic<uint64_t> g_quarantine_idx{0};
 
@@ -259,6 +265,12 @@
     ReportInvalidFree(ptr, __builtin_return_address(0), "on entry");
     return;
   }
+  // kuloPo/Kemu 8e1b5512 (apklayer 0022), after the chunk check above: malloc_usable_size reads the chunk header, which an invalid pointer must not
+  // reach (0006).
+  if (malloc_usable_size(ptr) > kMaxQuarantinedAllocationSize) {
+    __real_free(ptr);
+    return;
+  }
 
   // Defer the real free through the quarantine ring so a just-freed chunk (and
   // its Scudo region) stays live long enough that the translator's Arena cannot
